# Everything under server-mock-patches/ is applied or copied byte for byte, and
# the manifest pins a sha256 over those raw bytes. Any line-ending rewrite on
# checkout changes the hash and the build stops — a patch file fails as "patch
# does not apply", which reads like a stale patch set, and a copied file fails
# as "Copy source hash mismatch", which reads like nothing at all. On Windows
# core.autocrlf=true is the default that git for Windows installs, so without
# this a fresh clone there cannot build.
#
# Scope it to the directory rather than to *.patch: lib/dojo-features.js is
# hashed the same way, was not a .patch, and so went unprotected for two
# releases. A file added here later is covered without anyone remembering.
# -text: store and check these out byte for byte, whatever the platform.
server-mock-patches/** -text

# Everything the container executes as a script has the same requirement: a
# shebang line ending in \r makes the kernel look for an interpreter that does
# not exist ("bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M").
*.sh text eol=lf
